###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# The paper reports no training-cost or inference-cost figures.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - PFP is encoded as a trained model of the TeaNet method: the paper describes it as TeaNet with modifications (Morse-style two-body term, calculation-mode input, per-layer cutoffs), which the convention treats as settings and not as a new method. If PFP is to be a method of its own, that is one registry row.
# - Sizes of the PFP molecule and crystal data sets are given per structure type (Table S7) and not summed here; the data-generation cost (about 6e4 GPU days) has no property under schema 0.1.2 for a data set. The OC20 data-set node carries no reference calculation.
# - The force MAE of the HME21 benchmark and of Table S2 is the MAE of the norm of the force vectors, stated in the metric labels. Whether the HME21 errors are on the test split is implied, not stated.
# - Applications (Li diffusion barriers in LiFeSO4F, MOF adsorption, Cu-Au order-disorder, Fischer-Tropsch catalyst screening), silicon polymorph energies (Table S3) and the U-J table are not encoded.
# - Read from the arXiv v2 preprint, not the Nature Communications version.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: crossref).

